1759276800,0.053],[1761955200,0.007],[1764547200,0.0],[1767225600,0.011],[1769904000,0.001],[1772323200,0.0],[1775001600,0.0],[1777593600,0.004],[1780272000,0.0],[1782864000,0.002],[1785542400,0.002]]}],"frequency":"M"}],"qna":[{"question":"What does this measure?","answer":"Energy supplied is the electricity actually delivered to consumers in the month, in million units (MU), net of auxiliary consumption, all India. Peak demand is the highest instantaneous all-India demand in the month (in MW) and peak demand met is the part of it that was supplied. Shortage is (requirement minus supplied) as a per cent of requirement.^Energy requirement (the matching demand figure) is already on the dashboard as Energy Requirements."},{"question":"How should I read it?","answer":"Use the YoY view for energy (it removes the seasonal pattern). Peak demand reflects weather: it spikes in April-June and again in September when monsoon breaks, so compare the same month across years. Shortage is now below 1%, so supplied is almost equal to requirement; a rise in shortage is a stress signal."},{"question":"Definitions, revisions and caveats","answer":"CEA publishes each month's report with a lag of about two months and revises it ('Revised' in the report title). The series is built from the all-India row of each month's PDF; the parser checks that requirement minus supplied equals the reported shortage. The sign convention of the shortage columns changed over the years, so shortage here is recomputed as requirement minus supplied.^Reports before 2015 used slightly different state coverage (for example, the Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu merger), and Andaman-Nicobar and Lakshadweep are shown separately and are not in regional totals; the all-India row is used as published."}],"related":[],"layer":"added","scraped_at":"2026-10-30T07:11:26+00:00"}